      "Language": "jpn",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Increased osmolality of the airway surface fluid due to water loss associated with hyperventilation is considered to be a cause of exercise-induced asthma (EIA)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "We investigated the influence of changes in osmolality on airway epithelial ion transport in guinea pigs."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We explored from measuring open circuit potential difference in challenge with hypertonic saline or mannitol."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Diphenylamine-2-carboxylate (DPC), amiloride, and disodium cromoglycate (DSCG) prevented an increase of the potential difference (PD) after exposure of tracheal mucosa to 0.9-10.8% hypertonic saline solutions (HSSs) (p < 0.05, p < 0.01, and p < 0.01, respectively on 1.8%). An increase of the PD was observed after a single dose of 1.8% hypertonic saline solution (HSS) was applied to the mucosa, but no increase was observed after a single exposure to a 585 mOsm/kg aqueous mannitol solution (585AMS). The results remained the same when a Cl-free solution was used as the perfusate. The change of airway epithelial cell thickness was only suppressed significantly by DPC (p < 0.01) after 1.8% HSS challenge."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Not only the change of osmolality, but also changes of the Cl- and Na+ concentrations in airway surface fluid seem to have an important influence on the PD. cAMP-dependent Cl- channel may have a role, and the same mechanism may provoke EIA."
        }
      ]},
